WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.379
Transformers.

00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:05.029
Attention is all you need!

00:00:05.029 --> 00:00:07.069
Transformers beat state-of-the-art,

00:00:07.069 --> 00:00:09.800
transformers are better
than anything else in NLP!

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.800
Hi!

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.940
Are you tired hearing about transformer experiments
and you wish there was higher architectural

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:21.789
diversity when testing on benchmarks in machine
learning, but especially in natural language

00:00:21.789 --> 00:00:22.789
processing?

00:00:22.789 --> 00:00:26.119
Then congratulations, you found the right
video for you!

00:00:26.119 --> 00:00:32.439
Because today we are discussing this paper
that asks a very natural and interesting question:

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:35.280
“Are pre-trained convolutional models better
than

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:37.110
pre-trained transformers?”

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:43.290
From this paper, we will find out how CNN-based
models (whatever that might mean, we’ll

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:49.829
see in a bit), ... so CNN-based models, benefiting
from the same amount of pre-training as transformers,

00:00:49.829 --> 00:00:55.059
were found to be competitive or even outperforming
Transformers in certain experiments!

00:00:55.059 --> 00:00:57.770
Or at least that’s what the abstract of
the paper says…

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:02.930
Ms. Coffee Bean thinks that in this video,
you will learn something about transformers

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:10.360
versus CNNs, but you will also learn something
about the importance of framing in papers.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:11.390
Ms. Coffee Bean?

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.560
What even makes transformers so successful?

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.909
What do they have that other architectures
don’t?

00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:22.640
Well, many things:
First, they are efficient because things that

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.420
an RNN computed sequentially, transformers
compute in parallel, enabling large training

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:34.390
scales and faster computations on larger datasets
than LSTMs for example.

00:01:34.390 --> 00:01:39.510
Second and as a consequence, the pre-train
and fine-tuning paradigm came into fashion

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:40.510
in

00:01:40.510 --> 00:01:44.500
NLP, because with the transformer being more
efficient, pre-training became a thing with

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.710
language models on larger and larger amounts
of text.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:54.190
You know, we’re talking about BERT, RoBERTa,
T5, the GPT-family and so on.

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:59.950
Third, transformers enable contextual representations
through self-attention…

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:04.049
Ms. Coffee Bean, I must stop you there on
the "contextual" bit.

00:02:04.049 --> 00:02:09.289
Contextual representations in pre-trained
language models were not a first with transformers.

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:14.940
For example, ELMo and CoVE
enabled this already with LSTMs.

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:20.730
And also, pre-training and fine-tuning was
not invented with transformers.

00:02:20.730 --> 00:02:23.650
Pre-training was not that big of a thing in
NLP,

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:26.829
but certainly in computer vision, where basically

00:02:26.829 --> 00:02:30.029
every CNN backbone was pre-trained on ImageNet.

00:02:30.029 --> 00:02:36.959
So… this paper now asks: What if convolutional
architectures can actually perform on par

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:44.449
with transformers, but this hypothesis was
so far mostly neglected and untested, since

00:02:44.449 --> 00:02:49.389
transformers took all the attention! (pun
acknowledged, but not really intended).

00:02:49.389 --> 00:02:55.819
What if both the pre-training and the architectural
advances happened simultaneously with the

00:02:55.819 --> 00:03:02.299
transformer, but that this doesn’t mean
that these two factors shouldn’t be separated

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:04.109
and analyzed independently.

00:03:04.109 --> 00:03:06.629
And this “what if?”

00:03:06.629 --> 00:03:11.999
question, this possibility,
is what this paper aims to answer by…

00:03:11.999 --> 00:03:14.489
just trying it out with CNNs!

00:03:14.489 --> 00:03:17.260
Or that’s what the paper says…

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:22.510
There are recent works showcasing CNNs on
sequence to sequence modelling too, but this

00:03:22.510 --> 00:03:25.829
study is the first (Ms.Coffee Bean, did you
check?)

00:03:25.829 --> 00:03:27.909
to be more comprehensively structured

00:03:27.909 --> 00:03:33.439
on a range of tasks and focuses
on the pre-train-fine-tune paradigm.

00:03:33.439 --> 00:03:35.739
Or that is what the paper says…

00:03:35.739 --> 00:03:38.499
Ms. Coffee Bean, why do you keep saying this?

00:03:38.499 --> 00:03:39.499
Well...

00:03:39.499 --> 00:03:41.040
Why the focus on CNNs?

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:45.809
The paper says it:
Because their window is smaller than for usual

00:03:45.809 --> 00:03:49.969
self-attention, you know...
which is spanning all tokens, therefore they

00:03:49.969 --> 00:03:55.319
are faster to compute, not suffering from
quadratic time and memory complexity.

00:03:55.319 --> 00:03:56.739
It's only linear.

00:03:56.739 --> 00:04:02.729
Furthermore, they operate locally and do not
need this extra construct of positional encodings

00:04:02.729 --> 00:04:07.650
forced into the architecture
But this also means that the global context

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.329
cannot be captured by one layer only.

00:04:10.329 --> 00:04:16.980
In a convolutional network, it is the succession
of layers that increases the context window,

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:20.379
or the field-of-view, to use
a computer vision term.

00:04:20.379 --> 00:04:26.409
For example, for a kernel of the size of
two tokens, if the third token would be radioactive,

00:04:26.409 --> 00:04:28.800
let's say... in yellow,

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:34.689
it would mean that after one layer, the first
token does't include information from

00:04:34.689 --> 00:04:38.789
the third token,
but only from itself and the second token,

00:04:38.789 --> 00:04:44.210
but after a next layer, it also contains
information from the third token!

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:48.400
Okay, this is a very interesting striding
choice, Ms. Coffee Bean.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.060
Okay, but if we're going to replace things
with convolutions,

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:55.430
what about attention making the transformer
so powerful?

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.170
Well, does it really?

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:02.120
There have been papers recently, showing
that replacing learnt attention matrices with

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:07.750
random matrices does not hurt much and we
do not even want to start about papers replacing

00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:12.680
the self-attention layer with an MLP layer
or even with a Fourier Transform!

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:19.229
Ok, so to recapitulate the opponents of today’s
match: the CNN versus the Transformer!

00:05:19.229 --> 00:05:21.879
(Or this is what the paper says...)

00:05:21.879 --> 00:05:23.360
Let’s go!

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:29.680
In the right ring corner, we have the Transformer,
the well-known champion, the state-of-the

00:05:29.680 --> 00:05:30.680
art

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:31.850
in NLP beating T5!!…

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:36.460
Well, at least a T5 replica which is pre-trained

00:05:36.460 --> 00:05:43.270
on the Colossal Cleaned CommonCrawl Corpus
with span based masking as a pre-training

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:44.270
objective.

00:05:44.270 --> 00:05:50.360
Which in English means, that random spans
composed of more words are masked and the

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.190
model must fill in the blanks.

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:58.249
Then this model is further fine-tuned on
diverse downstream tasks comprising toxicity

00:05:58.249 --> 00:06:04.259
detection, sentiment or news or question classification,
semantic parsing...

00:06:04.259 --> 00:06:09.039
In the left corner, we have the challenger:
a sequence to sequence CNN model!

00:06:09.039 --> 00:06:15.849
Which… is basically a transformer architecture
but where self-attention is replaced with

00:06:15.849 --> 00:06:17.909
convolutional blocks?

00:06:17.909 --> 00:06:21.419
In other words,
this paper is perhaps framed as analyzing

00:06:21.419 --> 00:06:29.150
what gains the pre-train and fine-tuning paradigm
promises for CNNs versus transformers,

00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:36.780
but it is actually a “we replace the self-attention
with [insert here X]” and this X happens

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.440
to be convolutions.

00:06:38.440 --> 00:06:39.620
Wow…

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:44.289
I do not know about you, but Ms. Coffee Bean
thought that this paper was something different

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:49.699
than the MLP and FNet paper we have
seen lately, but in hindsight, it is exactly

00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:54.969
the same paper pattern, replacing the self-attention
layer with something else, this time with

00:06:54.969 --> 00:06:55.969
convolutions.

00:06:55.969 --> 00:06:56.969
It’s all about the framing.

00:06:56.969 --> 00:07:02.560
Therefore, keep in mind that this paper should
not be interpreted as: what would have happened

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:08.750
if the transformer did not ever come into
existence in NLP and completely different

00:07:08.750 --> 00:07:09.750
architectures

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:14.740
would have emerged, but rather as what would
happen if one layer in the transformer would

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:19.659
be not self-attentive, but convolutional,
where all the rest in the transformer

00:07:19.659 --> 00:07:20.919
is kept the same.

00:07:20.919 --> 00:07:24.590
Ms. Coffee Bean really believed
that this paper disentangles the transformer

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:28.069
architecture and the pre-train and fine-tune
paradigm.

00:07:28.069 --> 00:07:30.870
This paper does not really disentangle much...

00:07:30.870 --> 00:07:36.610
Okay, rant over, we will discuss this just
a little bit more at the end of the video.

00:07:36.610 --> 00:07:42.491
So, self-attention is replaced with convolutions
in three configurations: lightweight, dilated

00:07:42.491 --> 00:07:44.199
or dynamic convolutions.

00:07:44.199 --> 00:07:49.460
In short, these words mean the following:
A lightweight convolution is when convolution

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:53.559
parameters are shared every other channel.

00:07:53.559 --> 00:07:58.840
Dilated means that every other pixel counts
when computing the results of the operation

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:05.759
and dynamic convolutions are lightweight convolutions
but with learnable position-specific kernels.

00:08:05.759 --> 00:08:06.789
Yay.

00:08:06.789 --> 00:08:10.779
So this “CNN” (in quotes) model is trained
and

00:08:10.779 --> 00:08:13.430
fine-tuned on the same data as its

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:17.419
opponent, with the same masked span prediction
objective.

00:08:17.419 --> 00:08:20.400
And now, we come to the interesting part:

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:26.370
The authors let the two opponents compete
and well, the results on the tasks show

00:08:26.370 --> 00:08:30.050
that there is no clear winner here.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:35.450
There are cases where transformers are better,
but also many cases where a convolutional

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:40.640
variant is better, so this means that convolutions
also benefit from pre-training.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:41.880
That's acknowledged.

00:08:41.880 --> 00:08:47.530
That's even expected, because even the convolutional
architecture here is almost a transformer.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:53.130
The authors highlight that convolutions are
not the best choice in tasks that need cross-attention

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:58.490
between two or more sequences, like in natural
language inference where you must predict

00:08:58.490 --> 00:09:00.940
the relation between two sentences.

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:04.910
In this case, the cross-attention inductive
bias is desirable.

00:09:04.910 --> 00:09:10.260
On the bright side, experiments confirm the
theoretical considerations saying that

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:11.260
convolutions are faster.

00:09:11.260 --> 00:09:16.620
The scaling is linear in sequence length and
CNNs are in general more efficient in the

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.850
number of floating-point operations than transformers.

00:09:19.850 --> 00:09:24.519
In conclusion, the authors themselves say
that there is no clear winner in this battle.

00:09:24.519 --> 00:09:30.190
What Ms. Coffee Bean likes about this paper
is, that it shows that there is some architectural

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:34.230
tuning that might be beneficial on certain
tasks.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:39.440
The point of discussion for Ms. Coffee Bean
is on the last point made in the paper about

00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:44.110
disentangling pre-training and architectural
advances.

00:09:44.110 --> 00:09:51.060
The authors claim they showed that convolutional-based
architectures can also benefit from pre-training.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.269
And this is where Ms. Coffee Bean disagrees.

00:09:53.269 --> 00:09:58.800
So here she puts her point up for debate (she
might be wrong after all): To understand what

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:04.170
“CNN-based architecture” means here,
it also comes down to

00:10:04.170 --> 00:10:07.940
what “transformer architecture” means
in the first place.

00:10:07.940 --> 00:10:12.420
Is self-attention something of an essence
to transformers?

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:18.589
Is a transformer not a transformer anymore
if one replaces the self-attention with something

00:10:18.589 --> 00:10:23.079
else, be it an MLP, a Fourier Transform or
a convolution?

00:10:23.079 --> 00:10:28.020
Is it still a transformer without its residual
connections or normalization layers?

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.220
When is a transformer not a transformer anymore?

00:10:30.220 --> 00:10:35.010
Well, this is a hard question to ask for anything,
like when is Ms. Coffee Bean not Ms. Coffee

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.430
Bean anymore?

00:10:36.430 --> 00:10:37.990
If you remove the arms?

00:10:37.990 --> 00:10:38.990
Or the legs?

00:10:38.990 --> 00:10:40.500
Or the eyes?

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.839
This is a hard philosophical question
[whisper] essentialism.

00:10:43.839 --> 00:10:48.270
For Ms. Coffee Bean, self-attention is not
that much of an essence to the transformer,

00:10:48.270 --> 00:10:55.130
but it is this exact flow and parallelism
of information where one starts with a sequence

00:10:55.130 --> 00:11:02.550
or rather a set of tokens, then has an aggregating
or mixing layer for all the tokens to produce

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:03.959
new tokens.

00:11:03.959 --> 00:11:08.820
These newly produced tokens are then processed
individually and not together.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:13.620
And replacing the self-attention layer with
a convolutional one, does not make the architecture

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:15.830
immediately a CNN:

00:11:15.830 --> 00:11:18.839
It is perhaps a CNN-based transformer.

00:11:18.839 --> 00:11:23.880
But if we would name it such, then a transformer
is an attention-based transformer.

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.180
What do you think?

00:11:26.180 --> 00:11:32.040
What are you allowed to remove from the transformer
to still make it a transformer?

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:36.920
Are you happy with the framing of this paper
where they say that “CNNs” are compared

00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:43.060
with transformers or do you think that transformers
with convolutional layers are still transformers?

00:11:43.060 --> 00:11:48.320
And... it's terminology: how should the naming
of these things work anyway?

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:52.350
And with all these questions, Ms. Coffee Bean
leaves us...

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:53.240
Okay, bye!

